WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.934 --> 00:00:01.901
-Our first guest tonight is

00:00:01.901 --> 00:00:03.102
a Tony winning actor you know

00:00:03.102 --> 00:00:05.037
from films like
"Ferris Bueller's Day Off,"

00:00:05.037 --> 00:00:06.138
"Election," and "Glory."

00:00:06.138 --> 00:00:08.040
He stars in "Plaza Suite,"

00:00:08.040 --> 00:00:09.709
which is running
at the Hudson Theater

00:00:09.709 --> 00:00:12.178
for a limited engagement
through June 26th.

00:00:12.178 --> 00:00:13.312
Please welcome back to the show

00:00:13.312 --> 00:00:15.014
our friend Matthew Broderick,
everybody.

00:00:15.014 --> 00:00:16.983
[ Cheering and applause ]

00:00:16.983 --> 00:00:26.993 line:0%
♪♪

00:00:26.993 --> 00:00:31.898 line:0%
♪♪

00:00:31.898 --> 00:00:33.633
Welcome back.

00:00:33.633 --> 00:00:34.534
-Thank you.

00:00:34.534 --> 00:00:35.668
-It is great to have you here.

00:00:35.668 --> 00:00:37.770
It's great to have you back
on Broadway.

00:00:37.770 --> 00:00:39.238
-Yeah, I agree.
I'm great.

00:00:39.238 --> 00:00:42.242
[ Laughter ]
I'm very glad to be back
on Broadway.

00:00:42.242 --> 00:00:44.810
-So this is a play you get
to do with your wife,

00:00:44.810 --> 00:00:46.412
Sarah Jessica Parker.
-Exactly.

00:00:46.412 --> 00:00:48.380
-This was planned to happen
a while back

00:00:48.380 --> 00:00:50.650
and then it got derailed.

00:00:50.650 --> 00:00:52.919
-Yeah.
Two years ago,

00:00:52.919 --> 00:00:58.258
we started the show in Boston
and then traveled to New York

00:00:58.258 --> 00:01:01.460
and did our week of rehearsal
to get ready to open in New York

00:01:01.460 --> 00:01:05.464
and we were onstage
with full-on wigs and costumes

00:01:05.464 --> 00:01:10.069
and saw a lot of producers
started coming in

00:01:10.069 --> 00:01:13.439
and out of the theater
and whispering and chatting.

00:01:13.439 --> 00:01:16.909
And I think basketball
and hockey

00:01:16.909 --> 00:01:18.845
had just shut down
and we were like,

00:01:18.845 --> 00:01:20.013
"Why am I wearing this wig?"

00:01:20.013 --> 00:01:21.915
[ Laughter ]

00:01:21.915 --> 00:01:25.217
And, eventually, they were like,

00:01:25.217 --> 00:01:27.086
"We're going to take
a short break

00:01:27.086 --> 00:01:29.489
and let's all meet
in the house."

00:01:29.489 --> 00:01:31.824
And they said, "We're going
to stop the show for two weeks

00:01:31.824 --> 00:01:33.192
because of COVID.

00:01:33.192 --> 00:01:34.827
Might be four, honestly.

00:01:34.827 --> 00:01:36.396
[ Laughter ]
I'm not supposed

00:01:36.396 --> 00:01:38.564
to tell you that,
but it might be four.

00:01:38.564 --> 00:01:41.100
But it's two weeks."

00:01:41.100 --> 00:01:42.468
And then, you know,
two years,

00:01:42.468 --> 00:01:44.236
like for everybody else,
I guess.

00:01:44.236 --> 00:01:47.207
-So you were almost ready to go,
then you take two years off.

00:01:47.207 --> 00:01:49.275
Now, it should be noted
you play --

00:01:49.275 --> 00:01:51.277
you and Sarah play
three different couples.

00:01:51.277 --> 00:01:53.246
-Exactly.
-So this is a lot.

00:01:53.246 --> 00:01:55.181
You have a lot of dialogue
to learn.

00:01:55.181 --> 00:01:56.515
Did it come right back?

00:01:56.515 --> 00:01:57.750
-No, it did not.
-Okay.

00:01:57.750 --> 00:01:59.319
[ Laughter ]
-Yeah.

00:01:59.319 --> 00:02:00.620
I thought it would.
-Yeah.

00:02:00.620 --> 00:02:03.022
-I was like, "I'm not going
to study that stuff.

00:02:03.022 --> 00:02:04.858
Two years?
I know my -- You know.

00:02:04.858 --> 00:02:06.025
That's fine."
-Yeah.

00:02:06.025 --> 00:02:06.992
-And I got back and I was like,

00:02:06.992 --> 00:02:08.361
"This doesn't seem
familiar to me."

00:02:08.361 --> 00:02:11.297
[ Laughter ]

00:02:11.297 --> 00:02:12.832
-"Someone has switched out
my script."

00:02:12.832 --> 00:02:14.367
-"None of this is what was
in the script before."

00:02:14.367 --> 00:02:16.368
[ Laughter ]

00:02:16.368 --> 00:02:18.404
But then it gradually,
you know, it came back.

00:02:18.404 --> 00:02:19.738
-And then it's very exciting

00:02:19.738 --> 00:02:21.240
to do an opening night
on Broadway.

00:02:21.240 --> 00:02:22.976
Did opening night go well?

00:02:22.976 --> 00:02:25.211
-Yeah, it was thrilling,
you know.

00:02:25.211 --> 00:02:26.745
It was --

00:02:26.745 --> 00:02:30.483
You know, we have the exact same
crew and designers

00:02:30.483 --> 00:02:33.585
and everybody backstage
was there two years ago,

00:02:33.585 --> 00:02:35.488
so the fact that we all,
somehow,

00:02:35.488 --> 00:02:37.757
were still together and made it.

00:02:37.757 --> 00:02:39.758
You know, all the understudies
came out onstage

00:02:39.758 --> 00:02:42.428
and it was just --
it was a very lovely night.

00:02:42.428 --> 00:02:43.997
-Someone I knew was
at opening night

00:02:43.997 --> 00:02:47.033
and said there was a set mishap.

00:02:47.033 --> 00:02:48.100
-That's true.
-Yeah.

00:02:48.100 --> 00:02:50.703
[ Laughter ]
-Yeah, yeah.

00:02:50.703 --> 00:02:54.072
Well, the play, the third play,

00:02:54.072 --> 00:02:57.210
basically, the only story
of it is, is that the couple,

00:02:57.210 --> 00:02:59.379
and their daughter

00:02:59.379 --> 00:03:01.113
is supposed to be
getting married at the Plaza

00:03:01.113 --> 00:03:03.282
and she has locked herself
in the bathroom of the suite

00:03:03.282 --> 00:03:04.350
and will not come out.

00:03:04.350 --> 00:03:06.252
And the wedding
is about to happen.

00:03:06.252 --> 00:03:10.023
So they're banging on the door
and screaming and...

00:03:12.291 --> 00:03:13.959
...so the whole thing is that
she won't come out.

00:03:13.959 --> 00:03:15.461
She doesn't say anything
and at one point I say,

00:03:15.461 --> 00:03:18.798
"That's it, I'm going
to bust down the door."

00:03:18.798 --> 00:03:20.166
So I run to the other side
of the stage

00:03:20.166 --> 00:03:23.269
and I run full force
and I'm supposed to hit the door

00:03:23.269 --> 00:03:25.304
and it doesn't budge at all,
you know.

00:03:25.304 --> 00:03:26.973
[ Laughter ]
And that's, you know.

00:03:26.973 --> 00:03:28.374
And then I say
something amusing.

00:03:28.374 --> 00:03:32.511
[ Laughter ]
And so this time,

00:03:32.511 --> 00:03:34.847
I ran at the door and I just
smashed straight through it.

00:03:34.847 --> 00:03:38.684
[ Laughter ]
So,

00:03:38.684 --> 00:03:42.121
I was just in the bathroom,
suddenly.

00:03:42.121 --> 00:03:44.891
There was no daughter in there.
-Yeah. So that's jarring.

00:03:44.891 --> 00:03:45.792
[ Laughter ]
-Yeah.
-Yeah.

00:03:45.792 --> 00:03:47.926
-And it wasn't the kind of thing

00:03:47.926 --> 00:03:49.162
where you could improvise

00:03:49.162 --> 00:03:51.130
and say, you know,

00:03:51.130 --> 00:03:52.832
"Now, she's
behind the shower curtain."

00:03:52.832 --> 00:03:55.735
[ Laughter ]
I guess I could've said that.

00:03:55.735 --> 00:03:58.170
But I couldn't think
of what to say.

00:03:58.170 --> 00:04:00.272
-So what did you say?
-I think I said --

00:04:00.272 --> 00:04:02.975
and this is something
to keep in mind in any improv --

00:04:02.975 --> 00:04:04.843
-Yeah.
-"Let's pretend
that didn't happen."

00:04:04.843 --> 00:04:06.712
[ Laughter ]
Yeah.

00:04:06.712 --> 00:04:10.349
-Yeah. And I would imagine,
you know,

00:04:10.349 --> 00:04:12.217
obviously, you don't want things
to go wrong,

00:04:12.217 --> 00:04:14.186
but I imagine
that audience was thrilled

00:04:14.186 --> 00:04:16.623
-They were very delighted.
-to see a real moment like that.

00:04:16.623 --> 00:04:18.123
You obviously are onstage
with your wife

00:04:18.123 --> 00:04:19.825
and it's very nice.
Do you guys --

00:04:19.825 --> 00:04:23.629
Do you talk about the show
when you go home?

00:04:23.629 --> 00:04:25.264
-Not too much, you know.
-Okay.

00:04:25.264 --> 00:04:28.567
-I think we try to --
That could lead to some --

00:04:28.567 --> 00:04:32.237
You know, we try to avoid the,
"Would you mind terribly

00:04:32.237 --> 00:04:34.640
staying still
when I deliver this line?"

00:04:34.640 --> 00:04:37.643
[ Laughter ]
You know?

00:04:37.643 --> 00:04:42.415
Because that could lead
to a very difficult home life.

00:04:42.415 --> 00:04:44.283
[ Laughter ]

00:04:44.283 --> 00:04:46.352
-I mean, I would imagine
there's, you know,

00:04:46.352 --> 00:04:47.887
because, again, you're
communicating with the lines

00:04:47.887 --> 00:04:49.956
that Neil Simon
wrote all these years ago,

00:04:49.956 --> 00:04:52.157
so, if you want to communicate
beyond that,

00:04:52.157 --> 00:04:53.826
it has to be nonverbal,
which, you've been married

00:04:53.826 --> 00:04:55.327
a long time.
Nonverbal communication,

00:04:55.327 --> 00:04:57.029
I feel like my wife
and I crush that.

00:04:57.029 --> 00:04:58.197
[ Laughter ]

00:04:58.197 --> 00:05:00.332
-Yeah, we do a lot of that.

00:05:00.332 --> 00:05:03.302
I'm very not great at,

00:05:03.302 --> 00:05:07.440
if something happens onstage
that I don't think is

00:05:07.440 --> 00:05:08.807
as good as it should be
or something,

00:05:08.807 --> 00:05:12.945
I tend to do something to say,
you know, that happened.

00:05:12.945 --> 00:05:15.447
-Yeah.
-Like if I'll do something

00:05:15.447 --> 00:05:17.817
that always
gets a nice laugh, you know?

00:05:17.817 --> 00:05:21.521
And I'll do it and it's silence,
I'll be like --

00:05:21.521 --> 00:05:25.858
[ Laughter ]

00:05:25.858 --> 00:05:28.061
And sometimes I find myself
even being like --

00:05:28.061 --> 00:05:29.295
[ Laughter ]
I look at the audience.

00:05:29.295 --> 00:05:31.764
"What was wrong with that?"

00:05:31.764 --> 00:05:35.334
[ Laughter ]
And I kind of can't help it.

00:05:35.334 --> 00:05:37.870
Some actors have complained
about it like,

00:05:37.870 --> 00:05:39.605
"You're taking me out of it,
you know.

00:05:39.605 --> 00:05:43.008
[ Laughter ]
I'm in it. I'm in it," you know?

00:05:43.008 --> 00:05:44.309
-They're like,
"It also took me out of it

00:05:44.309 --> 00:05:45.678
when you ran
through the bathroom door."

00:05:45.678 --> 00:05:47.780
[ Laughter ]

00:05:47.780 --> 00:05:49.147
Your Broadway debut,

00:05:49.147 --> 00:05:51.283
how old were you when you did
"Brighton Beach Memoirs"?

00:05:51.283 --> 00:05:52.652
Which is also Neil Simon.

00:05:52.652 --> 00:05:53.553
-Twenty.
-Wow!

00:05:53.553 --> 00:05:55.655
-Yeah.
-Do you think back?

00:05:55.655 --> 00:05:57.690
Do you reflect back
on that time now, doing...?

00:05:57.690 --> 00:06:00.859
-A little bit.
I mean, it's 40 years ago

00:06:00.859 --> 00:06:02.995
-Wow.
-and here I am
in a Neil Simon play.

00:06:02.995 --> 00:06:06.198
And I have a little scene
where I lie on a bed.

00:06:06.198 --> 00:06:07.800
You know,
she's in the other room

00:06:07.800 --> 00:06:09.535
and I'm just on the bed
in the other room.

00:06:09.535 --> 00:06:12.871
And there was a scene
in "Brighton Beach"

00:06:12.871 --> 00:06:14.406
where, when other scenes
would happen,

00:06:14.406 --> 00:06:16.842
I'd be in my bedroom and the set
was that you wouldn't leave,

00:06:16.842 --> 00:06:20.512
so I was always lying on this
little foreshortened bed.

00:06:20.512 --> 00:06:22.414
And I'm back on a bed
[ Laughter ]

00:06:22.414 --> 00:06:24.684
and it's the same writer

00:06:24.684 --> 00:06:27.720
and I'm, you know,
incredibly old

00:06:27.720 --> 00:06:31.591
[ Laughter ]
and it's sort of amazing
to sit there.

00:06:31.591 --> 00:06:33.025
I mean, I don't know what
to make of it,

00:06:33.025 --> 00:06:34.961
but it does occur to me,
once in a while.

00:06:34.961 --> 00:06:39.098
-Do you remember that time
as a time that you were anxious

00:06:39.098 --> 00:06:41.667
and fearful of the weight
of doing a Broadway show?

00:06:44.403 --> 00:06:47.039
-Yeah, I definitely
was nervous, but,

00:06:47.039 --> 00:06:49.775
you know when you're 20,
you're kind of like,

00:06:49.775 --> 00:06:51.277
"I think I can," you know.
I don't know.

00:06:51.277 --> 00:06:53.212
I didn't get that worried,
for some reason.

00:06:53.212 --> 00:06:55.147
-That's good, though.
-Yeah.

00:06:55.147 --> 00:06:57.082
And, you know, this might be
a false memory.

00:06:57.082 --> 00:07:00.386
[ Laughter ]
It's possible, if I went back,
I was just like --

00:07:00.386 --> 00:07:01.987
You know, I was probably like,

00:07:01.987 --> 00:07:03.756
"Don't take me out there.
Don't take me out there."

00:07:03.756 --> 00:07:05.457
[ Laughter ]
-Talking to your old costars,

00:07:05.457 --> 00:07:07.059
it's like, "Was it weird being
around my confidence?"

00:07:07.059 --> 00:07:08.560
They're like, "No."
-"What confidence?"

00:07:08.560 --> 00:07:11.264
[ Laughter ]
Yeah, "I don't
remember it that way," yeah.

00:07:11.264 --> 00:07:13.399
I was so at ease,
don't you think?

00:07:13.399 --> 00:07:18.971
"No, I don't remember it
that way," yeah.

00:07:18.971 --> 00:07:20.405
-Can you tell nights
where there may be

00:07:20.405 --> 00:07:22.041
"Sex and the City" fans
in the audience

00:07:22.041 --> 00:07:24.877
because of how how drawn they
are to your wife?

00:07:24.877 --> 00:07:27.179
-Yeah, there's always
"Sex and the City" fans.

00:07:27.179 --> 00:07:28.680
[ Laughter ]

00:07:28.680 --> 00:07:31.984
Which is very lovely, you know.

00:07:31.984 --> 00:07:33.385
But there's a certain like,
"Whoo-hoo!"

00:07:33.385 --> 00:07:35.788
[ Laughter ]
it sounds like, you know,
maybe is that.

00:07:35.788 --> 00:07:40.192
Or, you know, if you hear
a wine glass go

00:07:40.192 --> 00:07:41.427
clunk, clunk, clunk,
clunk, clunk, clunk, clunk.

00:07:41.427 --> 00:07:44.029
[ Laughter ]
But that could be anybody.

00:07:44.029 --> 00:07:45.998
-That could be anybody.
Yeah. You shouldn't stereotype

00:07:45.998 --> 00:07:47.566
and just assume, yeah.
-I really shouldn't, no, no.

00:07:47.566 --> 00:07:49.001
-If it was a Cosmo.
If somebody brought

00:07:49.001 --> 00:07:51.636
their own Cosmo glass.
-Yeah. Yeah.

00:07:51.636 --> 00:07:53.505
-Did you watch?

00:07:53.505 --> 00:07:54.974
You know,
when someone's in a show,

00:07:54.974 --> 00:07:57.776
they might send you, you know,
the whole season of a show.

00:07:57.776 --> 00:08:01.047
Did you guys watch
and just like that all together?

00:08:01.047 --> 00:08:04.016
-No, I was not allowed to see
it ahead at all.

00:08:04.016 --> 00:08:08.087
-Why?

00:08:08.087 --> 00:08:11.289
-I was told so that it would be
more exciting for me.

00:08:11.289 --> 00:08:15.627
[ Laughter ]
-Okay.
-But I also think --

00:08:15.627 --> 00:08:18.364
I think there was a fear
that I would go to, you know,

00:08:18.364 --> 00:08:20.365
a Gillicuddy's on the corner
and say, "Hey,

00:08:20.365 --> 00:08:21.767
guess what happens?"

00:08:21.767 --> 00:08:23.635
[ Laughter ]

00:08:23.635 --> 00:08:25.404
-Oh, you'd have one too many
at the Irish pub

00:08:25.404 --> 00:08:28.240
-Yeah, that's what I mean.
-and you'd spoil just like that.

00:08:28.240 --> 00:08:29.775
-O'Donohue's, you know.

00:08:29.775 --> 00:08:32.778
[ Laughter ]
Yeah.

00:08:32.778 --> 00:08:35.347
So I wasn't trusted,
is what I'm trying to say.

00:08:35.347 --> 00:08:36.549
[ Laughter ]
-Gotcha.
-Yeah.

00:08:36.549 --> 00:08:38.384
-But it must be fun --
It must be fun --

00:08:38.384 --> 00:08:39.685
I mean, I would imagine --
to --

00:08:39.685 --> 00:08:40.952
to watch your wife
in that role again.

00:08:40.952 --> 00:08:42.621
It must've been so exciting.
-It was wonderful.

00:08:42.621 --> 00:08:44.323
And actually was fun
to not know.

00:08:44.323 --> 00:08:47.226
The big thing I had to not know
was the big secret of it,

00:08:47.226 --> 00:08:49.361
you know, so.
-Maybe she was worried that,

00:08:49.361 --> 00:08:50.830
if you saw that,
you'd, you know...

00:08:50.830 --> 00:08:54.066
-Well, she she did fool me.
Like I did not know.

00:08:54.066 --> 00:08:56.502
I went to the premiere and --

00:08:56.502 --> 00:08:59.238
I guess it's okay
to say now, but,

00:08:59.238 --> 00:09:01.306
a large character went [ Cluck ]

00:09:01.306 --> 00:09:04.276
[ Laughter ]
And I was like, "What?

00:09:04.276 --> 00:09:06.646
[ Laughter and applause ]

00:09:06.646 --> 00:09:08.247
What happened?"
-Does it worry you?

00:09:08.247 --> 00:09:10.516
Like I think I'd be worried
if my wife would be that good

00:09:10.516 --> 00:09:12.084
at keeping a secret.
Like I'd be like,

00:09:12.084 --> 00:09:14.152
"How did you?" You'd be like,
"What else are you?"

00:09:14.152 --> 00:09:15.388
Yeah.
-Yeah, yeah.

00:09:15.388 --> 00:09:18.124
"There's a lot you don't know
about me," yeah.

00:09:18.124 --> 00:09:19.591
[ Laughter ]

00:09:19.591 --> 00:09:21.794
It's possible.
-Well, I'm really, really happy

00:09:21.794 --> 00:09:23.895
to have you back here.
-I'm very happy to be back here.

00:09:23.895 --> 00:09:25.230
-It's great to have you onstage

00:09:25.230 --> 00:09:26.531
and thank you so much
for making time for us.

00:09:26.531 --> 00:09:27.966
[ Cheering and applause ]
-I love your show.

00:09:27.966 --> 00:09:29.968
-You guys, Matthew Broderick.

00:09:29.968 --> 00:09:32.371
"Plaza Suite,"
running at the Husband Theatre

00:09:32.371 --> 00:09:34.206
for a limited engagement
from June 26th.

